insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-finite-aquifer', 1, 'In engines/aquifer/aquiferInflux.js, what does the function pD(tD) compute?', '["The line source solution, one half of the exponential integral of 1/(4 tD), an expression that carries no reservoir radius and no aquifer radius at all", "The bounded circle solution for an aquifer with a no flow outer wall, obtained by Stehfest inversion of the scaled Bessel Laplace form at radius ratio reD", "The Lee and Wattenbarger rational fit to the finite radius infinite acting solution, the same fit the material balance engine uses inside its Carter-Tracy march", "The pseudo steady state asymptote, two tD divided by reD squared minus one, plus the natural logarithm of reD, minus three quarters of a dimensionless unit"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The source computes 0.5 times E1(1/(4 tD)), with the exponential integral evaluated by the Abramowitz and Stegun rational approximations. The producing body has been shrunk to a line of zero radius, so no geometry survives in the expression except the scaling carried by tD itself.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-finite-aquifer', 2, 'What solution does pDFinite(tD, reD) return, and how is it obtained?', '["A blend of the line source solution and the pseudo steady state line, joined by a hyperbolic tangent transition centred on four tenths of reD squared", "The constant terminal pressure influx function of Edwardson and others, fitted in two pieces that join at a dimensionless time of two hundred", "The van Everdingen and Hurst constant terminal rate solution for a bounded circle, evaluated by Stehfest inversion of the closed circle Laplace form", "The line source solution scaled by the ratio of aquifer pore volume to reservoir pore volume implied by the dimensionless radius ratio that was supplied"]'::jsonb, 2, 'It is the exact bounded circle solution, built from the well test radial model''s scaled Bessel Laplace form with a closed circle outer boundary at reD and inverted numerically. If a radius ratio of one or less is supplied the function falls back to the line source, which is itself evidence that the two are not interchangeable.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-finite-aquifer', 3, 'At tD 0.1 the ratio of the bounded solution to the line source is 25.2238601437433 at reD 2, 25.2235278833518 at reD 5, 25.2235278854392 at reD 20 and 25.2235278874633 at reD 200. What does that establish?', '["That the Stehfest inversion loses precision below a dimensionless time of one and returns nearly the same value whatever outer boundary condition it is handed", "That an aquifer smaller than about reD 3 is already in pseudo steady state at tD 0.1, so every radius ratio in the table gives the same dimensionless pressure", "That the two functions belong to the same solution family and differ only through the outer wall, which has not yet been reached at this early dimensionless time", "That the early time disagreement between the two functions has nothing to do with the outer boundary, since aquifer size spans a factor of a hundred with no effect"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A boundary effect cannot be indifferent to the position of the boundary. Six aquifers spanning outer radii from twice the reservoir radius to two hundred times it agree to seven significant figures, so what the ratio is measuring at early time is the cost of treating the reservoir as a point.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-finite-aquifer', 4, 'Where in dimensionless time do pD and pDFinite disagree most severely at reD 5?', '["At the moment the pressure disturbance first reaches the outer wall, which for a radius ratio of five occurs near a dimensionless time of ten", "At the earliest times, where the ratio at reD 5 reaches 25.2235278833518 at tD 0.1 and climbs to 400.729858461673 at a dimensionless time of 0.05", "At the latest times, where the bounded aquifer has reached pseudo steady state and its dimensionless pressure has begun to climb linearly with time", "In the middle of the transient near tD 5 to tD 10, where the bounded solution has felt the wall while the line source is still expanding freely"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The worst rows in the sweep are the first ones. The ratio is 400.729858461673 at tD 0.05 and 25.2235278833518 at tD 0.1, against 3.43706666433432 at tD 100, so the disagreement is far larger before any signal has been near the outer boundary than it is long afterwards.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-finite-aquifer', 5, 'Why is the line source solution worst at early dimensionless time?', '["The exponential integral approximation used by the engine is only accurate above an argument of one, so it is evaluated outside its range at small tD", "Water is still being produced from storage inside the wellbore at early time, so the influx that reaches the contact has not yet stabilised", "The reservoir radius has been removed from the problem, so a point of zero radius has no circumference to drain across and its computed drawdown starts at almost nothing", "The pressure disturbance has not yet reached the outer boundary, so the bounded solution behaves as though the aquifer were infinite and the two must agree"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Shrinking the producing body to a line throws the inner geometry away. A real reservoir of finite radius has been draining across its whole circumference from the first day, while the point source has not, so at tD 0.05 the line source sits 99.7504553307348 percent below the bounded value.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-finite-aquifer', 6, 'Measured against an effectively infinite aquifer, from roughly what dimensionless time onward is the line source within one percent of the bounded solution?', '["From about tD 53.5, having crossed five percent near tD 10.5 and two percent near tD 26.5", "From about tD 10.5, which is also where the five percent threshold is crossed for an aquifer of this size", "From about tD 0.25, after which the exponential integral is being evaluated in its well conditioned range", "From about tD 200, which is where the engine''s dimensionless influx function switches between its two fitted pieces"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A fine search against the bounded solution at reD 200 puts the five percent crossing near tD 10.5, the two percent crossing near tD 26.5 and the one percent crossing near tD 53.5. Convergence is far slower than most engineers assume, so the line source deserves a defence at any earlier time.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-finite-aquifer', 7, 'The Carter-Tracy routine in mbalEngine.ts carries its own infinite acting function rather than calling pD. What is that function a fit to?', '["The line source solution, reproduced as a rational polynomial to avoid evaluating the exponential integral inside a marching loop that runs many times", "The finite radius infinite acting solution, which it reproduces to better than 0.53 percent everywhere against the bounded solution evaluated at reD 200", "The pseudo steady state asymptote, extended backwards through the transient by a hyperbolic tangent weighting centred at four tenths of reD squared", "The dimensionless cumulative influx function WD, converted from cumulative influx to dimensionless pressure by dividing through by the aquifer influx constant"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Lee and Wattenbarger rational fit agrees with the bounded solution at reD 200 to 0.0253026204640359 percent at tD 0.1 and to 0.0000535441719464012 percent at tD 100, with a worst case of 0.520054132513312 percent. It keeps the inner boundary and drops only the outer one, so it is not a fit to the line source.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-finite-aquifer', 8, 'For a bounded circular aquifer, what is the pseudo steady state asymptote and what is its slope at reD 5?', '["One half of ln(tD) plus 0.80907, giving a slope that falls steadily as the dimensionless time increases through the late transient", "Two tD over reD squared, plus ln(reD), giving a slope of 0.08 and an intercept of 1.60943791243410 for an aquifer of this size", "The exponential integral of one over four tD, halved, which becomes a straight line in the logarithm of dimensionless time at late times", "Two tD over reD squared minus one, plus ln(reD) minus three quarters, giving a slope in dimensionless time of 0.0833333333333333"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The asymptote is exposed in the teaching lab as pssAsymptote(tD, reD) and drawn as the dashed line on the panel. Its slope is 2/(reD squared minus 1), which for a radius ratio of five is 2/24, and the linear growth is the aquifer depleting at constant rate.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-finite-aquifer', 9, 'At tD 100 and reD 5 the pseudo steady state asymptote gives 9.19277124576743 while the engine returns 9.30886079703705. How is the asymptote assembled?', '["As 200/24 plus ln(5) minus 0.75, that is 8.33333333333333 plus 1.60943791243410 minus 0.75", "As 200/25 plus ln(5), that is 8 plus 1.60943791243410, with no subtraction of three quarters applied at all", "As 100 times 2/24 multiplied by the group ln(5) minus 0.75, that is 8.33333333333333 times 0.859437912434100", "As 200/24 plus ln(5) plus 0.75, that is 8.33333333333333 plus 1.60943791243410 plus a three quarter correction"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The slope term is 2 times 100 divided by 25 minus 1, which is 8.33333333333333, and the intercept term is ln(5) minus 0.75, which is 0.859437912434100. Adding them gives 9.19277124576743 against the Stehfest value of 9.30886079703705, a difference of 1.26283520133346 percent.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-finite-aquifer', 10, 'At reD 5 the difference between the bounded solution and its pseudo steady state asymptote is 0.115787685376095 at tD 10, 0.116089551269612 at tD 100 and 0.116160994910047 at tD 1000. What converges?', '["The absolute difference converges towards zero, while the relative difference stays fixed at about 1.26 percent for every dimensionless time beyond the onset", "Only the relative difference converges, from 6.84012595710185 percent at tD 10 to 0.137970271308638 percent at tD 1000, while the absolute offset stays near 0.1161", "Neither converges, because the Stehfest inversion accumulates numerical error at a rate proportional to the dimensionless time being inverted", "Both converge together, since the bounded solution and the asymptote are two forms of the same expression once pseudo steady state has been reached"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The exact solution runs parallel to the asymptote, displaced upward by a nearly constant amount. The percentage closes only because the quantity itself grows linearly while that offset stays put, so the shorthand improves in relative terms without ever closing the gap.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-finite-aquifer', 11, 'Why does the group ln(reD) minus 0.75 appear both in the pseudo steady state asymptote and in the Fetkovich productivity index?', '["Because Fetkovich derived his productivity index by curve fitting the van Everdingen and Hurst tables, and the group is an artefact of that fit", "Because both expressions are written for a wedge aquifer, and the group carries the encroachment angle fraction that converts a full circle to a wedge", "Because it is the dimensionless resistance between the average pressure of a bounded circular body and the pressure at its inner boundary in pseudo steady state", "Because the constant three quarters converts reservoir barrels per day per psi into the dimensionless units that the bounded circle solution is expressed in, for any radius ratio"]'::jsonb, 2, 'One piece of geometry shows up in two places. Fetkovich needs it because his method assumes the aquifer is already in pseudo steady state, and the bounded circle solution needs it because it is the constant term of the same late time pressure profile. At reD 2 the group is negative, at minus 0.0568528194400547, which flags a geometry the form was never derived for.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-finite-aquifer', 12, 'The ratio of bounded to line source at reD 5 falls to a minimum of about 1.1118 near tD 6.06 and then rises again. What are the two branches?', '["Both branches measure the outer boundary, which first shortens the flow path and later restricts it once the aquifer begins to deplete at constant rate", "The falling branch is numerical error in the Stehfest inversion and the rising branch is the true physical difference between an unbounded and a bounded aquifer", "The falling branch is the transient period before the pressure signal reaches the wall, and the rising branch is the aquifer being recharged from beyond its own outer radius", "The falling branch is the point source idealisation dying out and the rising branch is the outer wall taking over, and the minimum is simply where one overtakes the other"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The two branches have nothing to do with each other. Dividing out the idealisation floor at tD 25 leaves a boundary contribution of 48.3166268015243 percent, and at tD 100 it leaves 241.873702563470 percent while the idealisation part has fallen to about half a percent. Nothing physical happens in the aquifer at the minimum itself.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-finite-aquifer', 13, 'At reD 20 and tD 100 the engine returns a bounded value of 2.74197106588156 and an asymptote of 2.74698540638607, so the dashed line sits above the curve. Why?', '["Pseudo steady state begins near four tenths of reD squared, which is 160 for this aquifer, so at tD 100 the transient is still running and has not finished", "The Stehfest inversion has not converged at this radius ratio, so the bounded value is slightly low and should be read as equal to the asymptote", "The asymptote always sits above the exact solution, and the ordering seen at reD 5 is the exception caused by that aquifer being unusually small in relation to its reservoir", "The encroachment angle has not been applied to the asymptote, so the dashed line is drawn for a full circle while the curve is drawn for a wedge"]'::jsonb, 0, 'An asymptote is a statement about late time, and late is measured relative to the size of the system. The onset of pseudo steady state moves out as the square of the radius ratio, so a large aquifer has not finished its transient at a dimensionless time where a small one finished long ago.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-finite-aquifer', 14, 'A finite aquifer is modelled with the infinite acting solution. Which way does the error run?', '["Late influx is understated, because a bounded aquifer holds extra water beyond the radius at which the infinite acting solution stops delivering anything at all to the contact", "Late influx is overstated, because dimensionless pressure is a pressure drop and influx moves inversely with it, so the smaller value delivers more water per psi", "Early influx is overstated and late influx is understated, so the cumulative influx over a long history is approximately correct", "Neither is affected, since the aquifer influx constant and the dimensionless time coefficient are unchanged by the outer boundary condition"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A bounded aquifer in pseudo steady state has the larger dimensionless pressure, because its value climbs linearly while the unbounded one flattens into a logarithm. Larger pD means more pressure charged for the same delivery, so dropping the boundary buys water the field never received.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-finite-aquifer', 15, 'Running Dake Exercise 9.2 with the mapped reD 5, then at reD 3, then infinite acting, gives 307.221409553720, 455.567695625077 and 156.177551848366 MMSTB with R squared values of 0.999975248425736, 0.999964546937325 and 0.863239485188882. What is the lesson about detectability?', '["Fit quality tracks the error faithfully in both directions, so the R squared value alone can be used to select the aquifer radius ratio without any independent mapping work", "Both wrong aquifers break the fit, so the correct radius ratio is the one that maximises R squared across the range of geometries the mapping allows", "An over sized aquifer breaks the fit and announces itself, while an under sized one leaves the fit intact and books more oil to replace the water it was denied", "Neither wrong aquifer affects the fit, because the regression has enough freedom to absorb any aquifer assumption into the estimated oil in place"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The infinite acting run falls to an R squared of 0.863239485188882 and cannot hide, but the reD 3 run holds 0.999964546937325 while sitting 46.0152870593195 percent above Dake''s answer. The asymmetry is the point, and it is why an aquifer radius ratio is a mapped quantity rather than a fitting parameter.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-carter-tracy', 1, 'In the van Everdingen and Hurst convolution as the engine implements it, what argument is the dimensionless influx function WD evaluated at for the increment applied at index j?', '["The dimensionless time of the current step, since every increment is carried forward to the present before the sum is taken over the whole history", "The dimensionless time elapsed since that particular increment, tD at the current step minus tD at index j, because every event has been running for a different length of time", "The dimensionless time of the increment itself, tD at index j, which fixes how much water that increment was capable of delivering in total", "The dimensionless time step between surveys, which for an evenly sampled annual history is the same constant for every increment in the sum"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The argument is a difference, not an elapsed total. Each pressure change is an event with its own start time, and the water it set in motion is still arriving, so the influx function has to be sampled at the age of that event rather than at the age of the field.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-carter-tracy', 2, 'How does the cost of the rigorous superposition grow with the number of pressure steps n?', '["As n, because each step adds exactly one evaluation of the dimensionless influx function to a running total that is carried forward", "As n log n, because the increments can be sorted by age and the older contributions grouped together once they have stopped changing appreciably", "As n squared exactly, so ten annual steps take one hundred evaluations and one hundred and twenty monthly steps take fourteen thousand four hundred", "As n(n+1)/2, which is 55 evaluations for the ten annual steps of Dake 9.2 and 7260 for the same ten years resampled monthly"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Step one needs one evaluation, step two needs two, and step n needs n, with no state carried between them. Resampling the same ten years from annual to monthly multiplies the work by 60.5 while a marching scheme over the same table multiplies it by twelve.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-carter-tracy', 3, 'Why can the engine''s van Everdingen and Hurst path not be used for the finite aquifer of Dake Exercise 9.2?', '["Its dimensionless influx function takes a single argument and carries no radius ratio, so the rigorous superposition here is an infinite aquifer method only", "Its centred pressure increments cannot be formed for an aquifer whose outer boundary reflects the pressure signal back towards the reservoir contact", "It requires strictly uniform time steps, and the Dake fixture uses calendar dates that include three leap years within the ten year production history", "It was superseded by Carter-Tracy in the same release that corrected the cumulative drawdown convention, and that older code path was deleted from the aquifer module"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The influx function implemented here is the Edwardson and others fit for an infinite aquifer, with no place for a dimensionless outer radius. In this codebase a bounded aquifer is reachable only through Fetkovich, which assumes pseudo steady state from the start, or through Carter-Tracy with the bounded pressure solution.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-carter-tracy', 4, 'The engine forms the superposition increments as centred differences. What is the first increment on the Dake 9.2 history, and why?', '["120 psi, the full pressure drop from 2740 psia to 2620 psia over the first year, applied at the start of the step where the drawdown begins", "172.5 psi, the centred difference formed from the year zero and year two pressures, which is the first increment the interior formula produces", "60 psi, because the leading increment is (pi minus p1)/2, a half increment by construction, which is worth remembering when a hand check disagrees by a factor of two", "0 psi, because the first survey is the initial condition and contributes no drawdown until at least one further survey has been recorded"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine uses the standard centred form, with the leading term a half increment and interior terms formed from the pressures either side. On this history the increments run 60.0000, 172.500, 210.500, 183.000 and so on, with the final survey contributing nothing because it has no forward half.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-carter-tracy', 5, 'How much state does the Carter-Tracy recursion carry from one step to the next?', '["The entire pressure history, since the cumulative drawdown at each step has to be recomputed from the initial pressure before the recursion can advance", "Two numbers, the cumulative influx and the dimensionless time of the previous step, with two evaluations of the pressure solution per step", "One number, the cumulative influx so far, because the dimensionless time is recomputed from the survey date at the start of every marching step", "Three numbers, the cumulative influx, the previous dimensionless time and the previous aquifer average pressure, which is updated the way Fetkovich updates it"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Carrying We from the previous step and the previous dimensionless time is enough to encode the whole history, which is exactly what the recursion buys over the convolution. The march evaluates the dimensionless pressure and its derivative once each per step, whatever the length of the history behind it.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-carter-tracy', 6, 'What is delta p at step n in the Carter-Tracy recursion as this engine implements it?', '["The cumulative drawdown, initial pressure minus the pressure at the current survey, not the change across the step and not a centred increment", "The pressure change across the step, that is the previous survey pressure minus the current one, applied at the midpoint of the interval", "The centred increment, half the difference between the pressures either side of the current survey, the same form the convolution uses", "The difference between the average aquifer pressure and the reservoir pressure at the contact, which is the quantity the Fetkovich inflow law uses at every step"]'::jsonb, 0, 'It is the total drawdown to date from initial pressure. The engine comments record that the centred increment was used here once, that the convention was corrected in 2026-05-17, and that the earlier form under predicted the influx on this benchmark by roughly eighty percent.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-carter-tracy', 7, 'Why is a cumulative drawdown correct in the recursion when the convolution needs increments?', '["Because the recursion evaluates the pressure solution at the current dimensionless time only, and that solution is defined for a single continuous drawdown event", "Because the aquifer forgets its earlier drawdowns once pseudo steady state has been reached, so that only the total drop from the initial pressure to the current survey remains relevant", "Because the two conventions differ by a constant factor of two that is absorbed into the aquifer influx constant when the geometry is converted to a wedge", "Because the accumulated effect of every earlier pressure change is already represented by the cumulative influx term in the numerator, so an increment would count the history twice"]'::jsonb, 3, 'In the convolution each term is a separate event and carries its own increment. In the recursion there is only one term, and the past enters through the stored cumulative influx, so feeding an increment as well accounts for the history twice on one side of the fraction and not at all on the other.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-carter-tracy', 8, 'In the Carter-Tracy numerator, U times delta p has the previous cumulative influx multiplied by the pressure derivative subtracted from it. What is that correction doing?', '["Converting the cumulative influx from reservoir barrels into the dimensionless units in which the bounded pressure solution and its time derivative are both expressed", "Removing the part of the influx that was produced back to surface as water, so that only the net imported volume drives the following step of the march", "Discounting the drive by the water that has already arrived, since water that has left the aquifer no longer has its own pressure support available to push more", "Correcting for the encroachment angle, because the influx constant is written for a full circle while the aquifer covers only a wedge of that circle"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The leading term is what the current total drawdown would draw from an aquifer that had so far delivered nothing at all. The subtraction turns that into what is left, which is why the increments shrink through a history even while the cumulative drawdown keeps growing.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-carter-tracy', 9, 'In the Carter-Tracy denominator, the previous dimensionless time multiplied by the pressure derivative is subtracted from the current dimensionless pressure. Why?', '["To convert the constant terminal rate solution into the constant terminal pressure solution the material balance requires at each of the surveys", "Because the response has already been running for that long, so the part of the resistance already consumed is removed and the memory of the history lives in that scalar", "To prevent the denominator from reaching zero at early dimensionless time, where the bounded pressure solution is small and the march would otherwise diverge", "Because the dimensionless time coefficient is defined on a different reference radius from the one used in the aquifer influx constant, so a rescaling of the step is needed"]'::jsonb, 1, 'If the clock started now, the resistance would be the current dimensionless pressure. It did not start now, and subtracting the elapsed part is what lets a single step stand in for the whole superposition sum behind it.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-carter-tracy', 10, 'A hand check of the first Carter-Tracy step on Dake 9.2 uses U 6445.68866666667 rb per psi, tD1 5.67218226008396 from a 365 day step, delta p1 120 psi and the bounded solution pD1 1.43968464638507. What does that step give, and why is it so simple?', '["3047427.44885635 rb, because both initial conditions vanish and the step collapses to tD1 times U times delta p1 divided by pD1", "773482.640000000 rb, because the numerator is the whole answer once the initial cumulative influx of zero has removed the correction term from it", "4387334.50909091 rb, because the dimensionless pressure drops out of the first step entirely when the previous step sits at zero", "1914087.86118418 rb, because the first step of any influx history is fixed by the leading half increment of 60 psi rather than by the full drawdown of 120 psi"]'::jsonb, 0, 'With no water yet imported the numerator correction vanishes, and with the previous dimensionless time at zero the denominator correction vanishes too. That leaves a rate times an interval, and it is the step to hand check whenever a march looks wrong. The engine''s own march is not this arithmetic: it spaces steps on the fixture dates, so the first step spans 366 days, and it uses its own infinite acting fit rather than the bounded solution, returning 3119439.46038555 rb.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-carter-tracy', 11, 'For the Dake 9.2 aquifer, what are the influx constant U and the dimensionless time coefficient, and what are they built from?', '["U is 211934253.721285 bbl and the coefficient is 0.001506 per day, being the maximum encroachable water and the reciprocal aquifer time constant respectively", "U is 116.496154838747 bbl per day per psi and the coefficient is 0.422897624804177, being a productivity index and a decay term over a 365 day step", "U is 6445.68866666667 rb per psi, from 1.119 times the angle fraction, porosity, total compressibility, thickness and reservoir radius squared, and the coefficient is 0.0155402253700930 per day", "U is 28413649389.1192 bbl and the coefficient is 0.388888888888889, being the full circle aquifer volume and the encroachment angle fraction that reduces it to a wedge"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The influx constant converts a psi of drawdown into reservoir barrels of capacity and the coefficient converts days into dimensionless time through permeability, porosity, viscosity, compressibility and the square of the reservoir radius. Take the coefficient from the aquifer module, which carries 0.00633; the material balance engine''s own Carter-Tracy march carries 0.006328 and gives 0.0155353153462794 per day, about 0.03 percent lower. The other quantities listed belong to the Fetkovich scheme, not to Carter-Tracy.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-carter-tracy', 12, 'What does the engine return for Dake Exercise 9.2 with the mapped finite aquifer?', '["OOIP 312000000 stb and We 89200000 rb, reproducing Dake''s published pair exactly, with an R squared of 1 and a mechanism of strong_water_drive", "OOIP 156177551.848366 stb and We 148248060.002236 rb, with an R squared of 0.863239485188882 and a mechanism of strong_water_drive at strong aquifer strength", "OOIP 532588241.588393 stb and no influx at all, with an R squared of 0.999317934436751 and a mechanism of depletion_drive, because the pressures are supported", "OOIP 307221409.553720 stb and We 88064588.3139400 rb, with an R squared of 0.999975248425736 and a mechanism of water_drive_with_depletion at strong strength"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Those are the two graded capstone quantities, 307.221409553720 MMSTB and 88.0645883139400 MMrb. The alternatives listed are what the same production table returns when the aquifer is taken to be infinite acting, or removed altogether, or simply asserted to equal the published values.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-carter-tracy', 13, 'The Dake 9.2 march runs from tD 5.68592541673827 at year one to tD 56.7505069599587 at year ten. What does that range mean for the aquifer?', '["The whole history sits below the pseudo steady state onset of four tenths of reD squared, so the aquifer is infinite acting throughout and the boundary is irrelevant", "Pseudo steady state begins at a tD of 10 for this radius ratio, so the march crosses into the bounded regime between year one and year two and stays there", "The history ends before the line source solution becomes a one percent representation, so an infinite acting march would have been within one percent throughout", "The dimensionless times exceed 200, where the engine''s influx function switches between its two fitted pieces, so the march spans a discontinuity in the fit"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The onset is at 0.4 times the square of the radius ratio, which is 10 here. Nine tenths of the history is spent in the bounded regime, which is why the finite solution matters so much on this case and why an infinite acting march goes so badly wrong on it.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-carter-tracy', 14, 'The drive indices on Dake 9.2 sum to 0.997165062762353 rather than to one. What accounts for the shortfall?', '["The least squares residual, since one value of the oil in place has to serve all ten surveys and no water has been produced, so the two denominator conventions coincide here", "The denominator convention, because the engine divides every index by gross withdrawal while the literature divides by the withdrawal net of the water produced back to surface", "The missing gas cap term, since the fixture sets the gas cap ratio to zero while the field clearly produced a rising gas oil ratio through the ten year history", "Rounding in the published PVT table, whose formation volume factors are quoted to three decimal places and whose solution gas ratios are quoted as whole numbers"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Cumulative water production is zero on this field, so gross and net withdrawal are the same number and the convention question cannot be responsible. The residual is positive at every survey, running from 533108.736888647 rb at year one to 597442.553453356 rb at year ten, which is a shape mismatch rather than noise.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-carter-tracy', 15, 'How should the Dake 9.2 verdict be stated?', '["The engine matches Dake, since 307.221409553720 MMSTB and 312 MMSTB agree to within a few million barrels on a field of this size and vintage", "The engine fails the benchmark, because the miss on the water influx and the miss on the oil in place are both in the same direction rather than offsetting", "The engine returns 307.221409553720 MMSTB against Dake''s 312 MMSTB, a difference of 1.53159950201266 percent, inside the recorded tolerance of 3.53 percent for this path", "The engine is within a tenth of a percent of Dake once the difference between his truth value and his own least squares fit of 310.2 MMSTB has been allowed for"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A verdict needs the reference, the quantity, the miss and the tolerance, and the tolerance is the part most often left out. Its size is justified by measured method spread: the two Carter-Tracy implementations in this repository differ by 2.17882338111103 percent on this very case, run on the same calendar spacing.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-combination-drive', 1, 'In the combination drive material balance, what does the gas cap expansion term Eg represent?', '["The reservoir volume of free gas that was liberated from solution in the oil zone over the pressure step", "The standard cubic feet of gas held in the gas cap divided by the standard cubic feet still dissolved in the oil", "The reservoir volume gained by the gas cap per stock tank barrel of oil in place, at a gas cap ratio of one", "The fraction of the total underground withdrawal that the expanding gas cap has supplied since the initial survey"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Eg = (Bti/Bgi)(Bg - Bgi) has units of reservoir barrels per stock tank barrel of oil in place. It is scaled by the gas cap ratio m to give the actual cap, so the unscaled term describes a cap whose reservoir volume equals the oil zone''s.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-combination-drive', 2, 'On Ahmed Example 11-1, the group Bti/Bgi works out at 1975.00000000000. What does that number mean physically?', '["A gas cap equal in reservoir volume to the oil zone would hold 1975 standard cubic feet of gas for every stock tank barrel of oil in place", "Each reservoir barrel of gas cap gas expands to 1975 barrels when it is produced to stock tank conditions at 14.7 psia", "The gas cap contains 1975 times more energy per unit volume than the oil zone at the initial reservoir pressure of 3000 psia", "The solution gas oil ratio would reach 1975 scf/stb if the pressure were lowered until the gas cap had fully redissolved"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Bti in rb/stb divided by Bgi in rb/scf leaves scf/stb, so 1.58/0.0008 is the standard cubic feet of cap gas per stock tank barrel of oil in place when the cap and the oil zone occupy equal reservoir volumes.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-combination-drive', 3, 'The gas cap of Ahmed Example 11-1 contributes 592500.000000000 rb of expansion. Which second route reaches the same figure without using Eg?', '["Multiply the cumulative produced gas of 1100000000 scf by the current gas formation volume factor of 0.00092 rb/scf", "Subtract the oil expansion NEo of 750000 rb from the net underground withdrawal of 1710200 rb and halve the remainder", "Divide the gas in the cap, 4937500000 scf, by the initial solution gas oil ratio of 1040 scf/stb and convert with Bti", "Multiply the cap''s initial reservoir volume mNBti of 3950000 rb by the expansion fraction Bg/Bgi minus one, which is 0.15"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The cap occupies 0.25 times 10000000 times 1.58 reservoir barrels initially, and the same gas occupies Bg/Bgi = 1.15 times that volume at 2800 psia, so its expansion is 3950000 times 0.15.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-combination-drive', 4, 'Why does the material balance express the gas cap as a ratio m of the oil zone volume rather than as its own gas in place volume G?', '["It avoids the need for a gas formation volume factor anywhere in the equation, which removes the largest PVT uncertainty", "It keeps a single in place unknown, since both hydrocarbon expansion terms then scale with N and m can be supplied from the map", "It is required for the drive indices to sum to one, which fails whenever a gas in place volume appears in the numerator", "It matches the convention used for the aquifer, which is also written as a multiple of the reservoir pore volume at initial conditions"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Writing the cap as m times the oil zone''s volume ties it to N, leaving one in place unknown. Two independent in place volumes multiplying terms that both scale roughly with pressure drop cannot be separated by a regression over a modest drawdown.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-combination-drive', 5, 'On Ahmed Example 11-1 the produced gas oil ratio Rp is 1100 scf/stb against an initial solution gas oil ratio of 1040 scf/stb. What does the gas term Np*Bg*(Rp - Rsi) = 55200.0000000000 rb account for?', '["The reservoir volume of gas remaining dissolved in the oil at the second survey pressure of 2800 psia", "The shrinkage of the produced oil between the initial pressure of 3000 psia and stock tank conditions", "The volume of gas that has been reinjected into the gas cap to support pressure during the reporting period", "The reservoir volume occupied by produced gas beyond what the produced oil originally held in solution"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Rp minus Rsi is 60 scf for every stock tank barrel produced, gas that was free in the reservoir rather than dissolved in the produced oil. At Bg = 0.00092 rb/scf across 1000000 stb that occupied 55200 reservoir barrels.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-combination-drive', 6, 'The underground withdrawal on Ahmed Example 11-1 is 1760200.00000000 rb. Which statement about that figure is correct?', '["It includes the 50000 rb of produced water, because produced water genuinely left the reservoir", "It excludes produced water, which is why it is smaller than the printed net withdrawal of 1710000 rb", "It counts only the oil and its dissolved gas, with free gas handled inside the gas cap expansion term", "It is the sum of the four drive index numerators, so it is the quantity the indices apportion between suppliers"]'::jsonb, 0, 'F = Np[Bt + Bg(Rp - Rsi)] + Wp*Bw, and the last term is 50000 stb of water at Bw = 1.0 rb/stb. The four drive index numerators sum to 1710200 rb instead, which is F less the produced water.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-combination-drive', 7, 'With N, m and the PVT all given, how is the water influx of Ahmed Example 11-1 obtained?', '["By regressing F/(Eo + mEg) against dp/(Eo + mEg) across the two available pressure surveys", "By subtraction: We = F - N(Eo + mEg + Efw), which leaves one unknown in the balance", "By marching a Fetkovich influx forward from the aquifer geometry until the cumulative matches the pressure history", "By assuming the water drive index equals the printed 0.2112 and multiplying it by the gross underground withdrawal"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Every other quantity in the balance is given or computable from the PVT, so the influx is a subtraction rather than a fit: 1760200 minus 1348918.75 gives 411281.250000001 bbl against the printed 411281 bbl.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-combination-drive', 8, 'Ahmed prints two influx figures for this case, 411281 bbl and 417700 bbl. What is the pair of numbers useful for?', '["Their average, 414490.5 bbl, is the recommended influx when the aquifer model has not yet been chosen", "Their ratio gives the fraction of the influx that arrived after the second survey rather than before it", "Their difference isolates N*Efw at 6418.75 bbl, testing the rock and water convention without trusting either figure alone", "Their difference is the produced water, confirming that the withdrawal was formed with the correct water formation volume factor"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The 417700 bbl variant drops the rock and connate water term entirely, so the gap between the two published values is exactly N*Efw. Misgroup the compressibilities or drop the (1+m) factor and the two variants no longer differ by 6418.75 bbl.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-combination-drive', 9, 'Why does the rock and connate water term carry a factor of (1 + m) in a reservoir with a gas cap?', '["The connate water saturation is higher in the gas cap than in the oil zone, so the water expansion has to be scaled up", "The compressing pore volume is the whole hydrocarbon pore volume, oil zone plus gas cap, and the cap is m times the oil zone", "The factor converts the formation compressibility from a gas cap basis to an oil zone basis before the two are added", "It compensates for the gas cap gas that redissolves into the oil as the reservoir pressure falls below the bubble point"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Efw is measured per stock tank barrel of oil in place, and the pore volume that compresses includes the gas cap. Since the cap is m times the oil zone''s reservoir volume, the term scales with (1 + m) for the same reason the gas term scales with m.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-combination-drive', 10, 'Drive indices apportion the net underground withdrawal A rather than the gross withdrawal F. What is A on Ahmed Example 11-1?', '["1760200.00000000 rb, the gross withdrawal including the produced water", "1348918.75000000 rb, the total hydrocarbon and rock expansion supplied by the reservoir", "411281.250000001 rb, the water influx that the balance requires the aquifer to have delivered", "1710200.00000000 rb, the gross withdrawal less the produced water"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A = F - Wp*Bw = 1760200 minus 50000. Subtracting the produced water from both sides of the balance leaves an identity whose left hand side is A, which is why dividing by A makes the four indices sum to one.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-combination-drive', 11, 'A set of drive indices for this case is computed on the gross withdrawal and sums to 0.971594137029883. What is the correct diagnosis?', '["The denominator is wrong: the shortfall is exactly Wp*Bw/F, which comes to 0.0284058629701170 here", "The pressure survey at 2800 psia is inconsistent with the reported cumulative production volumes", "The water influx has been underestimated by about 2.8 percent, which is within the noise of any aquifer estimate", "The gas cap ratio of 0.25 is too small, because a larger cap would raise the segregation index and close the books"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Dividing the same numerators by a denominator larger by Wp*Bw scales the whole sum by A/F. Here 50000/1760200 is 0.0284058629701170, exactly the shortfall, so the data is untouched and the denominator is the error.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-combination-drive', 12, 'Our water drive index computes to 0.211250877090399 and rounds to 0.2113, while the book prints 0.2112. What is the honest test?', '["Agreement to within one part in ten thousand relative, which is the precision the four printed decimals imply", "Agreement after both values are recomputed from the book''s rounded intermediates, which removes the discrepancy entirely", "Agreement to within one unit in the last printed place, which all four indices pass on the net withdrawal convention", "Agreement of the sum only, since individual indices are never reproducible when the author has rounded upstream"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The book divided a rounded influx by a rounded net withdrawal, so its fourth decimal carries upstream rounding. Asserting a tighter relative tolerance tests whether you rounded as the author did rather than testing the physics.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-combination-drive', 13, 'In mbalEngine.ts the per timestep field named sdi is computed as N*Efw/F. What does that field actually hold?', '["The segregation drive index from the expanding gas cap, which Ahmed also calls SDI", "The solution gas drive index, covering gas liberated from the oil below the bubble point", "The rock and connate water expansion index, the quantity Ahmed prints as EDI in this same example", "The sum of the rock, connate water and gas cap contributions, reported under a single combined heading"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine''s sdi carries the rock and connate water numerator, the quantity Ahmed prints as EDI, while the engine''s gdi carries the gas cap term Ahmed prints as SDI. The two names are crossed relative to the literature, so read the numerator rather than the acronym.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-combination-drive', 14, 'The engine''s drive index block divides every index by gross F, while its validation string for this benchmark claims the book convention with denominator F - Wp*Bw. How should that be read?', '["Both are true: the harness recomputes the printed indices in the book convention from the engine''s terms, while the runtime fields use gross F", "The validation string is stale, and the harness case for Example 11-1 has never been run against the printed indices", "The engine switches denominators automatically when produced water is present, so the two statements describe different runs", "The runtime fields are correct and the book convention is a legacy presentation that the harness retains for historical comparison"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The harness asserts the printed values by recomputing indices in the book convention from the engine''s per timestep terms. The runtime fields keep gross F, so a run and its own validation string describe two different denominators.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-combination-drive', 15, 'Why does neither of the engine''s two divergences from Ahmed''s index convention show up on the Ekene tank?', '["Ekene stays above its bubble point throughout, so no free gas exists and every drive index except the depletion index is zero", "Ekene has no gas cap and produced no water, so the crossed gas term is zero and the two denominators are the same number", "The Ekene fixture supplies per row PVT, which bypasses the drive index block and reports the book convention directly", "The Ekene regression is forced through the origin, which normalises the indices to sum to one before they are reported"]'::jsonb, 1, 'With m = 0 the gas cap field is zero, so the crossed naming has nothing to expose, and with Wp = 0 at every survey the gross and net withdrawals are identical, so the denominator choice has nothing to act on.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-history-matching', 1, 'How does a pressure history match differ from the Havlena-Odeh straight line solve on the same data?', '["It uses a weighted least squares regression in which the later surveys carry more weight, on the grounds that they sit further from the origin of the plot", "The pressure becomes the quantity being predicted rather than an input, and parameters are adjusted until the simulated pressures reproduce the observed ones", "It replaces the tank model with a multi cell simulation in which each cell keeps its own pressure and saturation history", "It solves for the water influx first and then recovers the oil in place by subtraction, avoiding a regression altogether"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The regression asks what oil in place best explains the voidage at the measured pressures. The match asks what pressure history a candidate set of parameters would produce, which is why the engine calls it the inverse material balance equation.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-history-matching', 2, 'What does simulatePressureHistory solve at each timestep?', '["A two parameter least squares problem in the oil in place and the aquifer water volume, refitted over every timestep from the initial survey to the current one", "A differential equation in time for the pressure decline rate, integrated forward from the previous step''s pressure", "A saturation balance that partitions the produced volumes between the oil zone and the gas cap before the pressure is updated", "A scalar equation in the unknown pressure, since the cumulative withdrawals at that step are already known from surface measurement"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The withdrawals are measured at surface and do not depend on the pressure, so F(p) = N*Et(p) + We(p) has one unknown. The engine brackets it between the initial pressure and a floor, then closes the bracket by safeguarded false position.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-history-matching', 3, 'Why does the simulator evaluate F and Et through the same per timestep code the regression uses?', '["So the PVT precedence chain of per row values, then lab table, then correlation is identical on both sides by construction", "So the drive indices reported by the match can be compared directly with those from a Carter-Tracy forward run", "Because the regression code is the only place where the water formation volume factor is applied to the produced water column of the input table", "Because a two point series is the only input shape the material balance engine accepts without a date column"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The simulator builds a two point series of the initial row plus a candidate row at the trial pressure and calls the ordinary per timestep routine. That keeps the forward and inverse paths in step on PVT without anyone having to maintain two implementations.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-history-matching', 4, 'Why do Fetkovich and Carter-Tracy aquifers require a fixed point iteration inside the simulator when a pot aquifer does not?', '["Their influx is expressed in stock tank barrels and has to be converted before it can enter the balance", "They need the encroachment angle applied at every step, which cannot be done inside a root search", "Their influx at any step depends on the whole pressure history rather than on the current pressure alone", "They are only defined for finite aquifers, so the outer boundary condition must be re-evaluated after each step"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A pot aquifer delivers influx as a pointwise function of pressure and can be solved inside the root search. The marching schemes cannot, so the simulator solves all steps with influx frozen, recomputes influx from the full simulated series, and repeats until the series settles within 0.2 psi or 25 sweeps pass.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-history-matching', 5, 'What is the residual at a fitted timestep in a history match?', '["The observed pressure minus the simulated pressure at that step, in psi", "The underground withdrawal minus the total expansion at that step, in reservoir barrels", "The observed cumulative influx minus the influx the aquifer model delivers, in reservoir barrels", "The difference between the matched oil in place and the regression estimate, expressed as a percentage"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Residuals are pressure differences in psi, taken over every timestep except the initial one and any excluded ones, and the reported root mean square error, maximum absolute error and sum of squares are all formed from those fit points only.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-history-matching', 6, 'Why does the matcher search on the natural logarithms of the parameters rather than on the parameters themselves?', '["It converts the material balance into a linear problem, which Levenberg-Marquardt requires before it can start", "It makes the pressure residuals normally distributed, which is the assumption behind the reported 95 percent confidence intervals on each parameter", "Every fitted parameter is strictly positive, and the log transform enforces that without special handling at zero", "It compresses the pressure range so that early and late surveys carry comparable weight in the objective"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Oil in place, aquifer volume, productivity index, radius and permeability are all positive quantities. Searching in log space keeps them positive automatically, and the bounds become a box in log space too.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-history-matching', 7, 'On a Fetkovich case where you do not choose a fit set, what does defaultHistoryMatchParameters give you?', '["Two free parameters: the oil in place and the aquifer water in place, with the productivity index held fixed", "One free parameter, the oil in place, because the aquifer is already constrained by its published geometry", "Four free parameters, adding the encroachment angle to the in place volumes and the productivity index", "Three free parameters: the oil in place, the aquifer water in place and the aquifer productivity index"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The default set is the in place scale plus the configured aquifer''s shape parameters. That is one parameter with no aquifer, two for a pot aquifer or Carter-Tracy, and three for Fetkovich, so accepting the default there frees three parameters without deciding to.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-history-matching', 8, 'You do not supply bounds for the oil in place. What box does the engine search in?', '["From zero to twice the starting value, a range chosen because it brackets any physically reasonable in place volume for a single tank", "From the starting value divided by 100 to the starting value times 100, a span of four orders of magnitude", "From the volumetric booking to the regression estimate, whichever of the two is the wider pair", "It searches unbounded, because the log transform already prevents the parameter from going negative"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Default bounds are wide multiples of the starting value: 100 either way for the in place scale and for aquifer permeability, 1000 either way for aquifer volume and productivity index, 30 either way for the Carter-Tracy radius. They are numerical guard rails, not physical statements.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-history-matching', 9, 'A matched parameter comes back flagged at_bound. What does that tell you?', '["The parameter converged to a physically meaningful limit, which is the strongest form of agreement available", "The optimiser ran out of iterations before it could move the parameter off its starting value", "The search wanted to move the parameter past the limit you set, so the reported value is the position of your constraint", "The parameter is strongly correlated with another fitted parameter, so the data identifies only their product and not either one on its own"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A parameter parked on a bound is not an estimate. Its value describes where you drew the line, and its interval is arithmetic from the curvature at a point on that wall. The run also warns that the bound is constraining the fit.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-history-matching', 10, 'How many non-excluded observations does the engine require before it will fit k parameters?', '["At least k + 1, so ten fit points permit up to nine parameters and six permit five", "At least 2k, so ten fit points permit five parameters and six permit three", "At least ten in every case, which is why short histories cannot be matched at all", "At least three per parameter, following the usual rule of thumb for regression degrees of freedom"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The guard is arithmetic and it is not the binding constraint. A smooth pressure decline sampled ten times is not ten independent facts, so the sensible number of free parameters is far below what the guard allows.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-history-matching', 11, 'A history match result carries both matched_ooip_stb and forward.estimated_ooip_stb. What is the difference?', '["The matched value is in stock tank barrels and the forward value is in reservoir barrels at the last survey pressure", "The matched value excludes any aquifer contribution, while the forward value includes the water the aquifer supplied", "They are always the same number, and the second is retained only for older callers that expect the original field name", "The matched value is the fitted parameter, while the forward value comes from a regression re-run on the observed pressures"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The forward run inside the result recomputes diagnostics at the matched parameters, but its oil in place still comes from the straight line regression against the observed pressures. On the Dake tank the two differ by 2977195.85917908 stb.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-history-matching', 12, 'Matching the Dake tank on the oil in place alone gives 310198605.412900 stb at a root mean square error of 4.31298999061806 psi. Freeing the reservoir radius as well gives 330212861.271306 stb at 0.533912409817980 psi. What is the lesson?', '["The two parameter match is the better answer because its lower error shows the fixed radius was wrong", "The better fitting match sits 6.45207796204173 percent above the one parameter answer and further from the published figure, so fit quality did not track correctness", "The one parameter match must be under converged, since a two parameter search cannot fit worse than a one parameter search", "The difference is within the tolerance of the capstone, so either value can be reported without qualification"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Dake''s own least squares answer for this exercise is 310.2 MMSTB and his stated truth is 312 MMSTB. The extra freedom improved the pressure fit by a factor of about eight and moved the oil in place 20014255.8584065 stb away from both.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-history-matching', 13, 'Freeing the reservoir radius gives 330212861.271306 stb with a 95 percent interval of 328414716.799611 to 332020850.988587, and freeing the aquifer permeability instead gives 305373480.952866 stb with an interval of 301547210.026266 to 309248302.649352. What do the non-overlapping intervals show?', '["One of the two runs failed to converge, since two correct matches of the same data must produce overlapping intervals", "The data contains a systematic error of about 25 million barrels that both matches have inherited from the pressure gauge", "The permeability match should be preferred, because a narrower interval always indicates the better constrained parameter", "A match interval measures curvature of the objective conditional on the model and fit set, so it cannot see that another parameter could have been freed"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Both runs converged, both freed two parameters, both quote a standard error under one percent, and their central values are 24839380.3184402 stb apart. The interval is a statement about the local shape of the misfit surface, not about the truth.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-history-matching', 14, 'A match returns residuals that are positive for the first seven steps and negative for the last three. What does that pattern tell you that the root mean square error does not?', '["The pressure gauge was recalibrated part way through the history, which is a data problem rather than a model problem", "The match has converged to a local minimum, and a different starting value would remove the sign change", "The misfit is systematic in time rather than scattered, which points at the timing of the modelled influx", "The oil in place is too high, because a run of positive residuals always indicates an overstated in place volume"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Noise alternates in sign. A run of one sign followed by a run of the other is the signature of a model whose shape in time is slightly wrong, and a summary statistic averages that structure away.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-history-matching', 15, 'What does the validation tier reported by a history match tell you about the match?', '["Nothing about the match itself, since the tier is inherited from the forward method''s benchmark record", "That the matched parameters have themselves been checked against a published benchmark case at the tolerance the reference states", "That the fit converged within the iteration cap and no parameter finished on a bound", "That the reported confidence intervals are valid at the 95 percent level for this particular run"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The tier and reference come from the forward run at the matched parameters, with a clause appended noting that the parameters came from a Levenberg-Marquardt minimisation of pressure residuals. A run that parked a parameter on a bound still reports the forward tier, so the warnings are what describe the run.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-validation-tiers', 1, 'The engine''s `resolveValidationTier` function decides which validation tier a material balance result reports. What does it take as its arguments?', '["The regression fit statistic, the drive index sum and the sign of the estimated oil in place at the final timestep of the run", "The fluid system, the aquifer model and whether a gas cap is present, and nothing about the data", "The complete production history, so that the tier can react to scatter in the pressure surveys it was handed", "The published reference case and the tolerance measured against it"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The resolver has exactly three inputs: fluid system, aquifer model and gas cap presence. No run data reaches it, so the tier is a property of the code path you invoked and is decided before the first survey is read.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-validation-tiers', 2, 'According to the engine''s own documentation of the tier vocabulary, what does `benchmark_verified` claim?', '["That the result on your screen has been checked and found correct to within the stated tolerance figure", "That this method is more accurate than the other aquifer paths in the engine, having been graded against each of them in turn", "That the implementation was tested against a published worked example and matched within a stated tolerance, with the reference recorded", "That the workflow includes documented assumptions, internal checks and calculation traceability"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The claim is about an implementation tested against a published worked example, matching within a stated tolerance, with the reference recorded for traceability. Nothing in it refers to the run in front of you.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-validation-tiers', 3, 'Across the twelve reachable combinations of fluid system, aquifer model and gas cap, which single path returns `published_method` rather than `benchmark_verified`?', '["The gas tank with no aquifer, the plain volumetric p over z straight line plot", "The oil tank with no aquifer, whose reference quotes a three way spread", "The oil tank with a Fetkovich aquifer, since Fetkovich and Hurst van Everdingen disagree", "The gas tank with Carter-Tracy, validated on the oil path rather than the gas one"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The volumetric gas p over z line is the only path with no committed worked example behind it in this engine, so it claims a recognized formulation with documented checks rather than a benchmark match.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-validation-tiers', 4, 'The tier type declares three values: `benchmark_verified`, `published_method` and `engineering_basis`. What is true of `engineering_basis` inside the material balance engine?', '["It is reserved for the history matching path, where the parameters are fitted from the pressure residuals rather than supplied by the user", "It is returned automatically whenever a run emits more than one warning about the structure or range of its input data", "It is returned for any aquifer model whose constants were defaulted by the engine rather than supplied on the case", "It is never returned at all, appearing only in the type declaration and two explanatory comments"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A grep of the engine finds the string four times: twice in comments and twice in type declarations. No return statement in the resolver produces it, so the vocabulary offers three levels while only two are reachable.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-validation-tiers', 5, 'The Ekene tank is run twice, once with the aquifer model set to none and once with a pot aquifer forced on, which returns an oil in place of -516449.043355256 stb. How do the two warnings arrays compare?', '["The forced run adds a warning that the computed aquifer volume of 42890161.1573930 rb is implausible for a tank of this size", "They are identical, both carrying the same five laboratory table sort complaints", "The forced run adds two warnings, one naming the fit statistic and one naming the drive index sum at the final timestep", "The forced run carries fewer warnings, since the aquifer absorbs the residual"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The diff between the two arrays is empty. Both runs emit the same five complaints about a descending laboratory PVT table, and the run that returns a negative volume of oil adds nothing at all.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-validation-tiers', 6, 'What happens to the reported `validation_tolerance_pct` when a pot aquifer is forced onto the Ekene tank?', '["It widens, because the engine recognises that adding an aquifer model gives the regression a second free parameter", "It is cleared entirely, because the pot regression returned a result the committed benchmark case does not cover", "It stays empty, since neither the correct path nor the forced one carries a stated tolerance in its payload", "It tightens from empty to 0.13 percent, the narrowest stated tolerance in the engine"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The correct path, oil with no aquifer, states no tolerance at all. The forced pot path is anchored on Pletcher''s multicell tables and states 0.13 percent, so the run returning a negative volume advertises the tighter claim.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-validation-tiers', 7, 'The Dake Exercise 9.2 performance history is run with the aquifer model set to none, giving an oil in place of 532.588241588393 MMSTB against a Carter-Tracy answer of 307.221409553720 MMSTB. What does the warnings array contain?', '["A single note recording that the aquifer parameters were defaulted", "One warning, that the drive index sum of 1.00423320400820 has drifted from unity", "Nothing at all, on any of the five categories of run check the oil path applies", "Two warnings, one naming the fit statistic and one naming the drive mechanism classification"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The run is completely silent. The fit statistic sits well clear of the 0.95 threshold, the index sum misses one by less than the 0.05 threshold, no aquifer default was substituted and no correlation was consulted.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-validation-tiers', 8, 'The oil path warns when the drive index sum at the final timestep differs from 1 by more than 0.05. Why does that check pass on the Ekene run with a pot aquifer forced on?', '["Because the pot plot degenerates to a single point on this fixture, so the fitted pair satisfies the row equation identically and the sum reads 1.0000000000000004", "Because the closure check is skipped for any run in which the user has explicitly selected an aquifer model", "Because the sum is formed from the regression residuals rather than from the per timestep terms, so it reads one by construction", "Because negative drive index values are clamped to zero before the sum is formed, which hides the depletion and rock expansion terms that both went negative on this run"]'::jsonb, 0, 'On this fixture both expansion terms are exactly linear in the drawdown, so the pot plot''s horizontal coordinate holds at about 42153.048 and all six points coincide. The fit has no leverage, and its output closes the row equation identically.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-validation-tiers', 9, 'The Dake case is run with Carter-Tracy but with the radius ratio removed, so the infinite acting solution is applied to a finite aquifer. What does the engine report?', '["Nothing, because the tier for the Carter-Tracy path is unchanged and no run check has a threshold that this crosses", "One warning, that the cumulative influx has exceeded the encroachable water the stated aquifer geometry can supply", "Two warnings, the first naming the missing radius ratio and the second naming the influx that resulted from its absence", "One warning, that the fit statistic of 0.863239485188882 is low and the data may have scatter or the wrong drive mechanism"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The fit statistic falls below the 0.95 threshold and the engine says so. Note what triggered it: the fit degraded, not the answer. A silent run elsewhere in this module was further from the truth than this noisy one.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-validation-tiers', 10, 'Which physical plausibility check does the material balance engine apply on the oil path?', '["A check that the estimated oil in place is positive, which fires ahead of every other diagnostic in the run", "A check that the cumulative water influx does not exceed the aquifer''s encroachable water at the final timestep", "None. The one negative volume check in the engine, on a pot regression returning a negative aquifer volume, sits on the gas path", "A check that the oil in place is within an order of magnitude of the volumetric booking supplied alongside the case"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The oil path runs closure, fit, correlation range, viscosity range and lab table structure checks. None of them tests whether the answer is possible, and the single negative volume warning in the file belongs to the gas path.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-validation-tiers', 11, 'The Carter-Tracy provenance string records an engine oil in place of 301.0 MMSTB against Dake''s 312 MMSTB, a 3.53 percent error, validated on 2026-05-17. What does the same case return on the current build?', '["301.0 MMSTB, unchanged, which is why the string has never needed refreshing", "307.221409553720 MMSTB, so the disagreement with Dake is now 1.53159950201266 percent", "312 MMSTB exactly, which is why the tolerance field is no longer populated on that path", "295.4 MMSTB, so the disagreement with Dake has widened past the tolerance the string states"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The code moved and the string did not. Of the six numbers the reference reports, the rock and connate water index is the only one that still rounds to its recorded value, leaving aside the gas cap index, which is zero on a tank with no gas cap and was never going to move. The stated tolerance is now conservative rather than descriptive.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-validation-tiers', 12, 'A colleague quotes a `benchmark_verified` tier with a stated tolerance of 3.53 percent and concludes that the field''s oil in place is therefore within 3.53 percent of the truth. What is wrong with that?', '["The tolerance belongs to the benchmark comparison, on the published author''s data, not to a different case", "Nothing, provided the same aquifer model and the same solution family were used on the field as on the benchmark", "The tolerance should be doubled first, because the benchmark compared against a single published value rather than a range", "The tolerance is a regression fit statistic in disguise and cannot be read as an accuracy under any circumstances"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A tolerance is an agreement measured on one specific comparison against a published case. Carrying it across to your own reservoir converts a statement about a code path into a claim about data the benchmark never saw.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-validation-tiers', 13, 'The provenance string for the oil path with a pot aquifer and a gas cap names the drive index convention as the book''s, with denominator F minus Wp times Bw. What does the runtime drive index block actually do?', '["It divides by net withdrawal, exactly as the string describes, so the two are in agreement", "It reports both conventions in separate result fields so that the consumer can choose between them", "It divides every index by gross withdrawal F and folds the produced water into the water index numerator", "It divides by gross withdrawal only when water production is zero and switches to net withdrawal otherwise, which is why the string is ambiguous"]'::jsonb, 2, 'On Ahmed Example 11-1 that gives a water index of 0.205250113623452 while the string prints 0.2112. The string describes a test that was run, not the contents of the field sitting beside it.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-validation-tiers', 14, 'The oil path with no aquifer, which ran the whole Associate tier, comes back `benchmark_verified`. What is unusual about its payload?', '["It reports a stated tolerance of 10 percent, the widest figure anywhere in the engine''s tier mapping", "It asserts a match within a stated tolerance while carrying no tolerance value at all", "It reports a tier but omits the reference string entirely, so the published source cannot be traced by a reviewer", "It reports a different tier depending on whether the gas cap ratio supplied with the case is greater than zero"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The tier word promises a stated tolerance and the payload does not carry one, while its reference quotes an engine result of 291.3 MM STB against a graphical fit of 257 and a volumetric booking of 270.6.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-validation-tiers', 15, 'Why is `resolveValidationTier` exported so that a generator can dump the full mapping into a golden file for the application to read?', '["Because an earlier hand written mirror had drifted, still showing Carter-Tracy as `published_method` after promotion", "So that the tier mapping can be edited by product staff without anybody having to touch the engine source code", "Because calling the resolver directly is too slow to do on every render of the application''s pre run tier badge", "So that each deployed environment can pin its own set of stated tolerances to suit the audience it serves"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s own comment records the drift. It is why a tier shown on a screen may have been produced by something other than the code that computed your answer, and why you should quote the result payload.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 1, 'The Associate reading was seven steps and the Professional reading was three phases. What does the Expert reading add on top of both?', '["A second regression using a different estimator", "A requirement that every fluid property be measured rather than correlated before the terms are built", "A recorded counterfactual number for every decision that could reasonably have gone the other way", "A formal uncertainty range around the oil in place, propagated from the scatter in the pressure surveys"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The deliverable stops being an oil in place and becomes an oil in place plus the map of what else it could honestly have been. A reviewer cannot audit judgment, but can audit whether you knew what the rejected alternative was worth.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 2, 'The F over Et column carries two independent features. Which of them can only be caught by reconciling against an independent volumetric booking?', '["The level, since an instantaneous pressure proportional aquifer barely drifts the ratio at all", "The drift, since a lagging influx changes the ratio too slowly to be seen against survey noise", "The intercept, since a regression through the origin cannot report one for you to inspect", "Neither. A fit statistic above 0.999 rules out both of them, which is what the straight line test is for"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Drift catches a lagging aquifer because the water arrives on its own clock. A pot style aquifer delivers influx in proportion to the drawdown, so the ratio stays almost flat and simply sits at the wrong height, and nothing inside the column detects that.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 3, 'On a constructed 12000000 stb tank carrying a 5000000 rb pot aquifer, what does the F over Et column do across six surveys?', '["It climbs steadily by more than 12 percent, which is the classic lagging influx signature every diagnostic is built to catch", "It falls steadily, because the imported water suppresses the withdrawal faster than it suppresses the expansion", "It becomes erratic, which is what makes the fit statistic of 0.999999988185953 misleading on this dataset", "It moves by only -0.0375706439360688 percent while sitting 12.24 percent above the truth"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The fit statistic on that dataset is 0.999999988185953. Every straightness test passes, the ratio is effectively flat, and the answer is still about a million and a half barrels wrong because the level is off.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 4, 'The Dake Exercise 9.2 history run with Carter-Tracy and a finite aquifer gives 307.221409553720 MMSTB. What does the compulsory no aquifer counterfactual give?', '["156.177551848366 MMSTB, because removing the aquifer removes the water that was doing the oil''s work", "532.588241588393 MMSTB, which is 225.366832034673 MMSTB higher", "312 MMSTB, matching Dake''s own answer", "A refusal, because the engine will not run a Carter-Tracy geometry with the aquifer model set to none"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Ignoring the aquifer forces the regression to find enough oil to explain the pressure history on its own, so the booking inflates. That run also comes back with a fit statistic of 0.999317934436751 and no warnings whatsoever.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 5, 'On the Dake case the radius ratio is removed, so the infinite acting solution is used on a finite aquifer. Which pair of movements results, and why?', '["Influx falls and oil in place falls, because the unbounded solution delivers less water per psi at every dimensionless time", "Influx rises and oil in place rises together, since more imported water requires more oil to have been present initially", "Influx rises to 148.248060002236 MMrb and oil in place falls to 156.177551848366 MMSTB", "Influx is unchanged while the oil in place falls"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The unbounded solution overstates late time influx because its dimensionless pressure drop flattens into a logarithm while the bounded one climbs. Imported water that is not there does work the oil would have had to do, so less oil is needed to close the books.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 6, 'The capstone grades the finite aquifer dimensionless pressure drop at tD 100. What must the pD explorer''s only control be set to, and what happens if it is not?', '["It must read reD = 5, the Dake geometry. On reD 10 the tile reads 3.61406650389498, missing the 0.05 tolerance", "It must read reD = 2, the smallest bounded case offered, since the graded field is the tightest aquifer in the sweep", "It must read reD = 20, so that the bounded solution has converged onto the line source before the tile is read", "It does not matter, because the tile reports the pseudo steady state asymptote, which is independent of the radius ratio chosen"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Dake aquifer has an outer radius five times the reservoir radius. The graded field fails on the selector rather than on arithmetic, so that control is the first thing to check when the number misses.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 7, 'At reD 5 and tD 100 the pseudo steady state asymptote is 9.19277124576743. How does the bounded circle solution sit relative to it?', '["Below it by about 1.3 percent, rising toward it as the remaining transient decays away", "Exactly on it, which is what pseudo steady state means once the boundary has been felt everywhere", "Far above it, by a factor of about three, which is the ratio against the line source solution instead", "Above it by 0.116089551269612, which is 1.26283520133346 percent, converging from above"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The asymptote is 200 divided by 24 plus the group ln(reD) minus 0.75, which is 0.859437912434100 at reD 5. The Stehfest inversion sits slightly above that straight line and closes on it as the last of the transient dies.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 8, 'On Ahmed Example 11-1, 1000000 stb of oil and 50000 stb of water were produced, with a two phase factor of 1.655 rb/stb, a gas factor of 0.00092 rb/scf and an initial solution gas ratio of 1040 scf/stb. What are the gross and net withdrawals?', '["Gross 1710200.00000000 rb and net 1660200.00000000 rb, the produced water having been netted twice over", "Gross 1760200.00000000 rb and net 1710200.00000000 rb, against the book''s printed 1710000 rb", "Gross 1655000.00000000 rb and net 1605000.00000000 rb, since the produced gas ratio equals the initial solution ratio", "Gross 1760200.00000000 rb and net 1760200.00000000 rb, because the water term belongs in the influx rather than the withdrawal"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The withdrawal is 1000000 times the bracket 1.655 plus 0.00092 times 60, plus the 50000 rb of produced water. Subtracting the produced water gives the net figure, which agrees with the printed value to 0.0116959064327485 percent.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 9, 'Using the Ahmed 11-1 water influx of 411281.250000001 bbl, how is the graded water drive index formed?', '["Divide the influx by the gross withdrawal without subtracting anything, giving the index the engine''s own fields report", "Divide the influx by the oil in place of 10000000 stb, converting to reservoir barrels with the initial two phase factor", "Subtract the 50000 rb of produced water and divide the remainder by the net withdrawal of 1710200 rb", "Subtract the produced water from the net withdrawal first, then divide the influx by what is left of it"]'::jsonb, 2, 'That gives 361281.250000001 divided by 1710200.00000000, or 0.211250877090399, which sits within one unit in the last place the book printed for its 0.2112.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 10, 'What does using gross withdrawal instead of net withdrawal cost on the two graded Ahmed 11-1 index fields?', '["The water index reads 0.205250113623452, missing its 0.002 tolerance threefold, and the sum misses by fourteenfold", "Only the sum fails, since the individual indices are unaffected by the choice of the common denominator used", "Both fields still pass, because the tolerance of 0.002 was set wide enough to absorb either convention", "The water index fails while the sum passes, since the four indices are rescaled by the same factor and still close to one"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The water index moves by 0.00600076346694700 against a 0.002 tolerance. The four indices then sum to 0.971594137029883 rather than 1, which is 0.0284058629701170 away, and that sum is exactly the ratio of net to gross withdrawal.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 11, 'The tank explorer''s drive indices sum tile reads 1.00000000000000 on the Ekene tank. Why does the denominator convention question never arise there?', '["Because the engine switches to the net withdrawal convention automatically whenever the tank has no aquifer model", "Because the tile reports the sum after normalising the four indices, so it reads one for any input at all", "Because Ekene has no gas cap, and the two conventions differ only through the segregation term", "Because Ekene produced no water, so gross and net withdrawal are the same number and the two conventions coincide"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The conventions diverge only through the produced water term. Five modules of this course went by without the question arising, and Ahmed Example 11-1 produced 50000 stb of water, which is what separates them.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 12, 'Entry six of the reserves memo gives, for each decision, the alternative and what it costs in barrels. What rule governs that entry?', '["The alternatives must be drawn from the same published source as the method actually used in the reading", "Each alternative must have been run rather than estimated, and must be one a competent peer would actually have chosen", "Each alternative must be quoted as a percentage of the headline answer so that readers can compare across fields", "At least three alternatives must be given, so that a reviewer can see the shape of the uncertainty around the answer"]'::jsonb, 1, 'An estimate written where a run belongs looks like evidence and is not. Straw man alternatives chosen because they happen to be close are the other way the entry can be filled in dishonestly.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 13, 'Which of these has no place in a defensible material balance memo?', '["The stated tolerance of the benchmark, together with the disagreement you measured on the current build", "The pressure range of the surveys and where the bubble point sits relative to it", "A validation tier offered as evidence that the case was set up correctly for this reservoir", "The level of the precedence chain that supplied each of the fluid properties the calculation used"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A tier is a claim about a code path checked against somebody else''s data. It says nothing about whether the path suited your reservoir, which is exactly the question a memo is written to answer.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 14, 'The Dake field had produced 77.43 MMstb by year ten. What recovery factors does that give against the Carter-Tracy answer and against the no aquifer answer?', '["25.2033216410527 percent and 14.5384358785452 percent", "24.8173076923077 percent and 25.2033216410527 percent, the difference being Dake''s own answer against the engine''s", "Both come to about 25 percent", "14.5384358785452 percent and 49.5781878276447 percent, which are the bounds set by the two aquifer counterfactuals"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Same oil out of the ground, two denominators, and more than ten percentage points between the answers. Recovery factor is the input to infill drilling, flood expansion and abandonment decisions, so the error does not stay where it started.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 15, 'What is the first check a competent reviewer makes on a reservoir simulation model, and what does it mean for material balance?', '["Whether the pressure match is within two percent, which supersedes the tank model once the grid has been history matched", "Whether the model''s oil in place agrees with the tank closed independently, so material balance becomes the check rather than the answer", "Whether the grid resolution is fine enough near the wells, which material balance cannot speak to in any case", "Whether the relative permeability curves were measured on core from this reservoir rather than taken from an analogue field"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A model that history matches beautifully while holding the wrong volume is the failure mode of the discipline. Simulation has thousands of parameters where a tank model has two or three, so a good fit means correspondingly less.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 1, 'In the aquifer influx solutions, pD is a dimensionless pressure drop. How does water influx respond to it?', '["Influx moves inversely with pD, so a larger dimensionless pressure drop means less water per psi of drawdown", "Influx moves in proportion to pD, since a larger drop means a stronger driving force at the contact, which is the reading the well inflow analogy suggests and the one to unlearn", "Influx is independent of pD, which only sets the timing of the response and never its size", "Influx moves with the square of pD, because the pressure disturbance spreads radially through the aquifer"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This is the direction most often stated backwards. A finite aquifer of small radius ratio depletes once the signal reaches its boundary, so its pD keeps climbing, and the climb is exactly why it stops delivering water.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 2, 'The engine carries two dimensionless pressure functions. What are they?', '["The same bounded solution evaluated with and without an outer boundary term added to the argument, the boundary term vanishing as the radius ratio grows without limit", "A constant terminal rate solution and a constant terminal pressure solution of the same bounded geometry", "An early time approximation and a late time approximation of one derivation, blended across the transition", "A line source solution that treats the reservoir as a point, and a bounded circle solution obtained by Stehfest inversion"]'::jsonb, 3, 'They are different solution families, not one family with and without a boundary. That is why they disagree most where the point source idealisation is weakest rather than where the boundary matters most.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 3, 'At what dimensionless time do the line source and bounded circle solutions disagree most, and by how much at reD 5?', '["At late time, around tD 100, where the bounded solution reaches 9.30886079703705 against a line source value of 2.70837365292708", "At tD 25, where the two families cross over", "They never disagree by more than a factor of two anywhere in the physically meaningful range of dimensionless time", "At early time, around tD 0.1, where the ratio between them is 25.2235278833518"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Early time is where the point source idealisation is worst, and the boundary has nothing to do with it. The late time divergence is real too, but it is a factor of about three and a half rather than twenty five.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 4, 'Where do the two solution families come closest at reD 5?', '["In the mid transient, at tD 5, where the ratio is 1.11701268357594", "At tD 0.05, before the pressure disturbance has travelled far enough for either boundary condition to matter", "At tD 50, on the way to the asymptote", "Nowhere. The gap widens monotonically with dimensionless time across the whole sweep the panel offers"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The point source idealisation has stopped hurting and the bounded aquifer has not yet felt its outer edge everywhere. That mid transient window is the only place a line source result is a reasonable stand in for a bounded one.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 5, 'How do the two families behave at late dimensionless time, and why?', '["Both flatten into logarithms, since every radial solution becomes logarithmic once the transient has decayed", "The line source climbs linearly while the bounded solution flattens, which is why line source results are conservative", "Both climb linearly, at slopes set by their respective outer boundary conditions and the fluid compressibility", "The bounded solution climbs linearly as the aquifer depletes, while the line source flattens into a logarithm"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A bounded aquifer reaches pseudo steady state and every point in it falls at the same rate, giving a linear climb. A point source in an infinite medium never runs out, so its response grows only logarithmically.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 6, 'The pseudo steady state asymptote for a bounded circular aquifer is 2 tD divided by (reD squared minus 1), plus a further group. What is that group, and where else does it appear?', '["ln(reD) minus 0.75, the same group that sits in the denominator of the Fetkovich productivity index", "ln(reD) on its own, which also appears in the steady state radial inflow equation used for wells", "ln(reD) minus 0.5, the group that appears in the constant terminal pressure van Everdingen solution", "reD squared minus 1, which is also the ratio of aquifer volume to reservoir volume for a wedge geometry"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The group is the signature of pseudo steady state flow in a bounded circular system, and it appears in the Fetkovich productivity index for exactly the same physical reason: the aquifer has stopped behaving transiently.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 7, 'At tD 100 the bounded solution gives 2.74197106588156 at reD 20 against a line source value of 2.70837365292708. What does that comparison establish?', '["That the Stehfest inversion becomes numerically unreliable once the radius ratio exceeds about ten", "That a large enough aquifer is still effectively infinite acting at that dimensionless time, the two differing by 1.24 percent", "That the two families always converge at late time, whatever the radius ratio, given enough dimensionless time", "That the pseudo steady state asymptote no longer applies once the radius ratio passes ten, since the aquifer never depletes within any dimensionless time a real field will ever reach"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Whether a boundary matters depends on whether the pressure signal has reached it. At reD 5 the same comparison gives a factor of about three and a half, because a small aquifer has felt its edge long before tD 100.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 8, 'What are the consequences of using the infinite acting solution on an aquifer that is genuinely finite?', '["Late time influx is understated and the oil in place is correspondingly overstated by the regression, since a bounded aquifer delivers more water per psi than an unbounded one", "Both the influx and the oil in place are overstated, since the model has more water available to it", "Late time influx is overstated and the oil in place is understated, because imported water does work the oil would have had to do", "Only the timing changes, since the total influx over the life of the field is fixed by the aquifer volume"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The unbounded pD flattens while the bounded one climbs, so the unbounded solution keeps promising water after the real aquifer has run down. The regression then needs less oil to close the books.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 9, 'What does the engine recover from the Dake Exercise 9.2 performance history using Carter-Tracy with a finite aquifer?', '["312 MMSTB with an influx of 89.2 MMrb, reproducing Dake''s own published answer exactly, the finite treatment having been tuned against that exercise", "301.0 MMSTB with an influx of 92.4 MMrb, which is the pair the provenance string records for this path", "307.221409553720 MMSTB with an influx of 88.0645883139400 MMrb, at a fit statistic of 0.999975248425736", "532.588241588393 MMSTB with no influx reported, since the finite treatment suppresses the aquifer term entirely"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Those two values are the first two fields the Expert capstone grades, in MMSTB and MMrb respectively. The engine reports both in single barrels, so the unit conversion is the usual reason the fields miss.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 10, 'Dake''s own answer for the Exercise 9.2 reservoir is 312 MMSTB. How far is the engine from it?', '["3.53 percent, the tolerance the string states", "10 percent, the tolerance stated for the Fetkovich path on the same underlying reservoir data", "1.53159950201266 percent, well inside the stated tolerance", "Zero, since the engine was tuned against this exercise when the finite aquifer treatment was introduced"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The stated tolerance of 3.53 percent was measured in May on an earlier build. Re running the case today gives a smaller disagreement, so the recorded tolerance is now conservative rather than descriptive.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 11, 'How does the engine classify the Dake Exercise 9.2 case, and what do the drive indices read?', '["Depletion drive with no aquifer strength, indices 0.984393260149972 and zero, summing to 1.00423320400820", "Strong water drive, indices 1.04254388249892 and a negative depletion term, summing to exactly one", "Combination drive with a gas cap, since the produced gas ratio climbs from 650 to 1160 scf/stb over the history, which the engine reads as a segregation contribution", "Water drive with depletion at strong aquifer strength, indices 0.567843338103932 and 0.417877131928747, summing to 0.997165062762353"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The shortfall of 0.00283493723764727 is a genuine residual in the influx solution rather than a denominator artefact, since this history produced no water. It sits well inside the engine''s 0.05 closure threshold.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 12, 'What problem does the Carter-Tracy method exist to approximate away?', '["Converting between constant terminal rate and constant terminal pressure boundary conditions at each timestep", "Interpolating the van Everdingen and Hurst tables, which were published only at discrete dimensionless radii, which the Lee and Wattenbarger polynomial fits were introduced to avoid", "Estimating the aquifer''s productivity index when the encroachment angle and permeability are both unknown", "Superposing the response to every past pressure change, which makes the work grow with the square of the run length"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Exact superposition also means every step has to be recomputed when an early one changes, which makes iterative work impractical. Carter-Tracy replaces the sum with a recursive step that carries the history forward.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 13, 'What does Dake''s Table 9.3 performance history show over its ten years?', '["Pressure falling from 2740 to 1460 psia while cumulative oil reaches 77.43 MMstb and the produced gas ratio climbs from 650 to 1160 scf/stb", "Pressure held nearly constant by the aquifer while cumulative oil reaches 77.43 MMstb and the gas ratio stays at 650 scf/stb", "Pressure falling from 3000 to 2800 psia over a single step, with 1000000 stb of oil and 50000 stb of water produced", "Pressure falling from 2740 to 2029 psia over four annual steps, matching the printed influx table of the Fetkovich example, which is the geometry the Fetkovich benchmark uses rather than this one"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The pressure falls hard despite strong water support, which is what water drive with depletion means. The climbing produced gas ratio is the other signature, and it is what makes the two phase formation volume factor move over the history.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 14, 'The Carter-Tracy provenance string records a correction to the delta p convention made in the same release as the validation. What was it?', '["The convention moved from the midpoint pressure across each step to the end of step pressure, doubling the first step, which had been the source of a systematic overstatement of the influx", "The convention moved from an averaged step drop to the cumulative drop from initial pressure, correcting a systematic under prediction of influx of about 80 percent", "The convention moved from gauge to absolute pressure, which had been suppressing the influx at low reservoir pressures", "The convention moved from the cumulative drop to an averaged step drop, which improved agreement with the published exercise"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The string records the correction alongside the validation numbers, which tells a reviewer that the path was unstable at the time of the test. That is the kind of scope clause worth reading before quoting the tolerance.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 15, 'Ahmed Example 11-1 produced 1000000 stb of oil and 1100000000 scf of gas. What is the produced gas oil ratio, and why does it matter?', '["1100 scf/stb, and it enters through the gas cap expansion term rather than through the withdrawal", "1040 scf/stb, the initial solution gas ratio, since the reservoir was still above its bubble point over the step, so no free gas was produced at all", "850 scf/stb, the solution gas ratio at the end of the step, which is the value the withdrawal term uses", "1100 scf/stb, and it enters the withdrawal term through the difference between it and the initial solution gas ratio"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The gas that came out of the reservoir beyond what was dissolved in the produced oil at initial conditions is free gas, and the withdrawal term prices it at the current gas formation volume factor.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 16, 'On Ahmed Example 11-1 the two phase formation volume factor moves from 1.58 to 1.655 rb/stb and the gas factor from 0.0008 to 0.00092 rb/scf. What are the oil and gas cap expansion terms?', '["Eo of 0.100000000000000 rb/stb from the change in the oil factor, and Eg of 0.150000000000000 rb/stb", "Eo of 0.0750000000000000 rb/stb and Eg of 0.237000000000000 rb/stb", "Eo of 0.075 rb/stb and Eg of 0.025 rb/stb", "Eo of 0.237000000000000 rb/stb and Eg of 0.0750000000000000 rb/stb, the gas term being the smaller of the two"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The oil term is the change in the two phase factor. The gas cap term is the initial two phase factor divided by the initial gas factor, multiplied by the change in the gas factor, which is why it is so much larger.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 17, 'The rock and connate water expansion term on Ahmed Example 11-1 comes to 0.000641875000000000 rb/stb. Which grouping produces it?', '["The initial two phase factor times (1 plus m), times the initial water saturation applied to the sum of both compressibilities, divided by (1 minus Swi), times the drawdown", "The initial two phase factor times Swi times (cw plus cf), divided by (1 minus Swi), times the drawdown, with no gas cap factor", "The initial two phase factor times (1 plus m), times the sum of Swi times cw and the formation compressibility on its own, divided by (1 minus Swi), times the drawdown", "The initial two phase factor times (1 plus m), times (cw plus cf), divided by Swi, times the drawdown across the step"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The connate water compressibility is weighted by the water saturation while the formation compressibility is not, and the (1 plus m) factor is there because the gas cap pore volume also contains connate water and rock.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 18, 'Solving the combined material balance for the influx on Ahmed Example 11-1 with the oil in place given as 10000000 stb gives what result?', '["361281.250000001 bbl, the influx net of produced water", "417700.000000000 bbl, the value the book prints for its own back calculation of the influx", "411281.250000001 bbl, against the book''s printed 411281 bbl", "1710200.00000000 bbl, since the influx must balance the whole of the net withdrawal at this point"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The influx is the withdrawal minus what the oil, the gas cap and the rock and water expansion could supply between them. It is the third of the Ahmed fields the Expert capstone grades, in barrels.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 19, 'The book also prints an influx of 417700 bbl for the variant that neglects rock and fluid expansion. What does the difference between the two tell you?', '["That the two calculations used different produced gas ratios, the larger one having assumed all produced gas was free, which would change the withdrawal rather than the expansion", "That 6418.75 bbl is what the rock and connate water expansion supplied, confirming the (1 plus m) factor and the compressibility grouping", "That the book''s rounding accounts for the gap, since both figures are quoted to the nearest hundred barrels", "That the expansion term contributes about 1.5 percent of the total withdrawal on this case"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Isolating the term this way is a check on the convention rather than on the arithmetic. If your expansion grouping is wrong, this difference will not reproduce and you will know before the indices are formed.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 20, 'The engine field named `sdi` and Ahmed''s printed SDI on Example 11-1 do not refer to the same quantity. What is the relationship?', '["The engine''s `sdi` holds the rock and connate water expansion, which the literature calls EDI, while the engine''s `gdi` holds the gas cap term the literature calls SDI", "The engine''s `sdi` holds the gas cap term while Ahmed''s SDI holds the rock and connate water term, so the two labels are simply swapped in place", "They are the same quantity, differing only in the denominator each convention divides by", "The engine''s `sdi` is the sum of the segregation and expansion contributions, which Ahmed prints separately as SDI and EDI, so the engine figure would always exceed either printed one"]'::jsonb, 0, 'On a tank with no gas cap the confusion is easy to miss, because the gas cap term is zero and the engine''s field carries the whole rock and water story. On a combination drive case it will cost you a comparison.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 21, 'Dividing the Ahmed 11-1 drive indices by gross withdrawal gives a sum of 0.971594137029883. What is that number?', '["The fraction of the withdrawal that the four identified mechanisms could account for, the remainder being unexplained by any drive present in the tank", "Exactly the ratio of net withdrawal to gross withdrawal, so it is a denominator artefact rather than a closure failure", "A closure failure of 2.8 percent, which is within the rounding the book applied to its printed intermediates", "The fraction that would close to one if the produced water were added back into the water drive index numerator"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Every index has been scaled by the same factor, so the sum is scaled by it too. When indices miss one on a case with produced water, check the denominator before you go looking at the data.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 22, 'The book prints its Example 11-1 indices to four decimals. How should an engine result be compared against them?', '["By requiring agreement to within one unit in the last printed place, since the book divided rounded intermediates", "By requiring a relative agreement of 0.1 percent or better, which is what four printed decimals implies about the author''s own precision", "By recomputing the book''s intermediates to full precision first, then asserting an exact match against the result", "By comparing only the sum, since the individual indices are too sensitive to rounding to be worth checking"]'::jsonb, 0, 'All four indices pass that test under the net withdrawal convention and all four fail it under the gross convention. A tighter relative tolerance would be asserting against the author''s rounding rather than against the physics.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 23, 'What does the engine''s history matching routine actually minimise?', '["The scatter of the F against Et points about the fitted straight line, using an iteratively reweighted regression over the surveys retained in the fit", "The difference between the matched oil in place and the volumetric booking supplied alongside the case", "The pressure residuals between observed and simulated history, by Levenberg-Marquardt on the inverse material balance", "The sum of the squared differences between the modelled and observed cumulative water influx over the history"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The forward calculation asks what the tank does given the parameters. The inverse asks what parameters would have produced the observed pressures, which is a far more powerful tool and a far more dangerous one.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 24, 'What are the default search bounds on the oil in place parameter in a history match?', '["A factor of ten either side of the volumetric booking, which must therefore be supplied with the case", "The interval from a hundredth to a hundred times the preliminary regression''s estimate", "A factor of two either side of the preliminary estimate, tightened further whenever the fit statistic is high", "No bounds unless the user supplies them"]'::jsonb, 1, 'That is four orders of magnitude of freedom by default, seeded from the preliminary regression. It is why the bounds themselves are a decision to record rather than a detail to accept.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 25, 'A history match finishes and the report flags a parameter as being at its bound. What does that mean and what should you do?', '["The bound is constraining the fit, so widen it or revisit the starting value rather than reading the result as a fitted answer", "The parameter has converged tightly, since the search settled on a well defined limit rather than wandering across the interior of the search space", "The bound was reached because the iteration cap was hit first, so the fix is to allow more iterations", "The parameter is not identifiable from the data and should be removed from the match entirely"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A value resting on a bound is not something the data chose. It is the edge of the box you drew, and the engine says so explicitly for exactly that reason.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 26, 'When does the engine call a history match quality poor?', '["When the fit statistic on the forward regression at the matched parameters falls below 0.95", "When the drive indices at the matched parameters fail to sum to one within 0.05", "When the root mean square pressure error exceeds 2 percent of the initial pressure", "When the maximum absolute pressure residual exceeds 50 psi at any point included in the fit"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The warning also names what to look at, which is the drive mechanism assumptions and the data quality, before the fitted values are trusted. A poor pressure match is a modelling problem before it is a data problem.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 27, 'A history match result carries both a `matched_ooip_stb` field and a full forward run. Which is the headline answer, and why?', '["The forward run''s estimate, since it reflects the complete diagnostics at the matched parameters, including the influx series and the drive index apportionment", "Whichever of the two is closer to the preliminary regression, since the match should not move the answer far", "Neither on its own. The two are averaged, which is why the engine reports both alongside each other", "The matched field, because the regression inside the forward run re-estimates the volume from the observed pressures"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The forward run is there for drive indices, the influx series and the diagnostics at the matched parameters. Reading its regression output as the match result quietly substitutes a different estimator for the one you asked for.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 28, 'Forcing a pot aquifer onto the Ekene tank gives an oil in place of -516449.043355256 stb at a fit statistic of 0.999485673716372. What is the general lesson for history matching?', '["That pot aquifers should never be used on undersaturated tanks, whatever the diagnostics suggest", "That a fit statistic below one indicates the extra parameter was unnecessary, which is the test to apply before any parameter is added to a match", "That negative results should be clipped to zero before the drive indices are formed, as the influx series already is", "That an added free parameter will be spent whether or not the physics needs it, and the fit statistic will not tell you"]'::jsonb, 3, 'One extra freedom on data that needed none was enough to move the answer by more than the entire tank. A model with thousands of parameters can reproduce any history at all, including a wrong one.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 29, 'What validation tier does a history match result report?', '["Always `engineering_basis`, since fitted parameters cannot be benchmarked against a published example", "The tier of the forward run at the matched parameters, with a sentence about the minimisation appended to the reference", "A tier one level below the forward run''s, to reflect the additional freedom the match was given, which is why fitted results carry a weaker claim", "No tier at all, the field being left empty so that a consumer cannot quote a benchmark claim over a fitted result"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The tier is inherited from the code path, exactly as it is for a forward run, so it says nothing about whether the match was well posed. The appended sentence is the only trace of the match in the provenance.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 30, 'The stated tolerances across the engine''s paths range from 0.13 percent to 10 percent. How should that spread be read?', '["As a quality ranking, the tighter tolerances marking the paths that were implemented most carefully", "As a measure of how much data was available for each benchmark, wider tolerances meaning fewer test cases", "As the width of the comparison each path was willing to accept, which depends on what the reference itself was", "As a confidence interval on the oil in place, so a 10 percent path should be quoted with 10 percent error bars around the volume the run reported"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A 0.13 percent claim against a numerical simulation and a 10 percent claim against a published field estimate are not comparable. A wide claim against a hard target can be better evidence than a narrow claim against a soft one.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 31, 'What does `published_method` claim that `benchmark_verified` does not, and what does it not claim?', '["It claims a peer reviewed formulation with documented assumptions and internal checks, and claims no comparison against an outside printed answer", "It claims a wider tolerance than a benchmark path, and claims nothing about the traceability of the calculation or about the assumptions the workflow rests on", "It claims the method is simpler and therefore safer, and claims nothing about the literature it came from", "It claims the implementation is provisional, and claims nothing about whether the workflow is documented"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Everything in the claim is about the code and its documentation. It is the honest word for having implemented what the literature says, with working that can be shown, where nobody has printed an answer to check against.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 32, 'Of the twelve reachable tier paths, how many carry a stated tolerance value in the payload?', '["All twelve, since the tier word asserts a match within a stated tolerance in every case", "Nine, the three without one being the volumetric gas line and the oil tank with no aquifer in both its gas cap variants", "Six, one for each distinct tolerance value the engine states across its paths", "Eleven, the only exception being the volumetric gas line, which is the one path that is not benchmark verified anywhere in the mapping"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The gas case is consistent, since a method claim has nothing to be tolerant about. The oil cases are not: they assert a match within a stated tolerance and state none, on the path that ran the whole Associate tier.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 33, 'The tier resolver returns a fourth field on the oil path with no aquifer, explaining that the least squares and graphical estimators are the dominant source of disagreement on real data. What happens to it?', '["It is appended to the reference string, which is why that string is the longest in the engine at nearly a thousand characters", "It is promoted into the warnings array whenever the fit statistic on that path exceeds 0.99", "It is never attached to a result, because the declared return type has three fields and the assembly copies three", "It is written to the golden tier file that the application badge reads, but not to the engine result"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The most useful caveat about the most heavily used path in the engine is present in the source and absent from the output. A provenance system is only as good as the fields that survive to the consumer.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 34, 'The oil Fetkovich path states a tolerance of 10 percent, while its reference string reports the influx table reproducing the printed column within 1 percent. What is the hazard?', '["The string is inconsistent with itself and one of the two figures must be a typing error in the source", "One tolerance number covers two claims of very different strength, so quoting it at the influx table understates your evidence tenfold", "The 1 percent figure applies only to the first marching step, after which the agreement degrades toward the stated 10 percent by the fourth annual step", "The 10 percent figure is the tolerance on the aquifer geometry inputs rather than on the result the path produces"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The payload carries the weaker of the two claims. Quoting the 1 percent figure at an oil in place would be the opposite error, and both come from reading the tolerance without reading the string it belongs to.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 35, 'A run comes back `benchmark_verified` with no warnings, a fit statistic of 0.9998 and a drive index sum of 1.004, reporting an oil in place three times the volumetric booking for the field. What should you do?', '["Accept the material balance answer, since the tier and the diagnostics all agree and the volumetric booking rests on a map", "Reject the volumetric booking and revise it upward, since two independent chains rarely disagree by that much without a mapping error", "Re run with a tighter convergence tolerance", "Investigate the model, since nothing in the tier or the run checks tests whether the answer is possible"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The tier is a lookup on the code path and the run checks test self consistency and input ranges. A silent, well fitting run on the Dake history was 225 MMSTB above the truth for exactly this reason.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 36, 'The Expert workflow records a counterfactual number for each decision. What does it forbid?', '["Recording a counterfactual that was not actually run, since an estimate written where a run belongs looks like evidence", "Recording more than one counterfactual per decision, since a reader cannot compare three answers at once", "Recording a counterfactual whose result is close to the headline answer, since that is not a meaningful alternative", "Recording a counterfactual for the fluid system, since that decision is settled by the geology rather than by the data long before the surveys are read"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The rule is what makes the map auditable. A reader can check whether a number came from a run, and cannot check whether a judgment was sound, which is why the runs are the part that has to be real.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 37, 'Why does an Expert reading record where the bubble point sits relative to the pressure range?', '["Because the drive index convention changes below the bubble point, the gas cap term becoming active", "Because the two phase factor equals the oil factor above it, so the oil expansion term is pure expansion and changes character below", "Because the engine refuses to run any case whose surveys straddle the bubble point without a laboratory table covering both sides of it", "Because the encroachment angle of the aquifer must be re measured once free gas appears in the reservoir"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Above it the solution gas ratio holds constant and the two factors coincide. Below it gas comes out of solution, the expansion term picks up a liberated gas contribution, and everything downstream of it moves.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 38, 'On the Ekene tank the rock and connate water expansion carries 39.2996108949418 percent of the total expansion at every survey, not just the last. Why is that share constant?', '["Because the engine normalises the two expansion terms against their sum before reporting either of them", "Because the pressure surveys are equally spaced in time, so each step contributes an equal share of the drawdown", "Because the water and formation compressibilities are constant, which fixes the ratio regardless of the oil term", "Because both expansion terms are linear in the drawdown on this fixture, so their ratio cannot depend on which survey you read"]'::jsonb, 3, 'It is the same structural fact that makes the drive indices identical at every timestep, and the same one that makes the pot aquifer plot degenerate to a single point when an unnecessary aquifer is forced onto this tank.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 39, 'You are handed an oil case with produced water whose drive indices sum to 0.97. What do you check first?', '["The pressure surveys, since a non representative static pressure is the commonest cause of a closure failure", "The aquifer model, since an influx term that is too small will leave the water drive index short of what the withdrawal requires it to supply", "The denominator convention, since gross and net withdrawal diverge as soon as water has been produced", "The PVT provenance, since a correlation standing in for a laboratory value will bias the expansion terms"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The two conventions agree exactly when no water has been produced, which is why the question never arises on a tank like Ekene. Chasing the data first on a case that has produced water will cost you a day.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 40, 'Three of the six Expert capstone fields are vulnerable to the same class of error. Which one?', '["Precision, since the tolerances are tight enough that a rounded entry will fall outside them", "Units, since two fields are graded in millions while the engine reports in single barrels and a third is graded in barrels", "Sign, since the aquifer counterfactuals can drive the reported volumes negative", "Convention, since all three depend on whether the indices were divided by gross or net withdrawal, which the third module spends a whole lesson on"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Every tolerance in that capstone is far wider than any rounding you could commit, so a miss is a setup error rather than a precision error. A factor of a million is one keystroke away on the first two fields.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 41, 'At its last survey the Ekene tank had produced 261475.039999678 stb against an oil in place of 12139208.1074968 stb. What is the recovery factor, and why does the denominator matter?', '["2.15397114609312 percent, and the denominator is the number every later recovery statement is a fraction of", "4.65 percent, and the denominator matters only for reserves reporting rather than for engineering decisions", "21.5397114609312 percent, and the denominator is fixed once the volumetric booking has been signed off", "2.15397114609312 percent, and the denominator can be revised freely later since recovery factors are quoted at a point in time"]'::jsonb, 0, 'An error in the oil in place does not stay there. It propagates into every quantity expressed as a fraction of it, which on the Dake history meant more than ten percentage points of recovery factor between two equally defensible aquifer models.'),
  ('mbal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 42, 'Which set of habits does the Expert tier intend you to carry into every course that follows it?', '["Plot before you fit, check the intercept, reconcile against volumetrics, and state the pressure range behind the answer", "Diagnose before modelling, model before defending, and never revise a written diagnosis once a fit is on the screen", "Prefer bounded solutions to unbounded ones, prefer measured properties to correlated ones, and prefer benchmarked paths to others wherever a choice between them exists", "Run and price the counterfactual, state the convention beside the number, re run the provenance rather than quoting it, and reconcile against something independent"]'::jsonb, 3, 'None of the four is about material balance. They transfer to displacement, to flood management, to simulation and to work that has nothing to do with tanks at all.');
